  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nCurrent synoptic features, along with a surface high pressure center \nto our south will promote for one more day of above normal \ntemperatures in the upper 60s and low 70s, with light winds on \nTuesday. This is going to change as the jet stream to our north \nbegins to dig southward into our region Tuesday night into \nWednesday. The jet digging to the south will promote the development \nof a much more meridional pattern, with the trough strengthening, \nand a ridge building to our west. As a result, the warm airmass that \nwe have been seeing, along with cold air advection in the Upper \nPlains will allow for the development of a low pressure center to \nbegin developing to our north. The center of the low will continue \nto deepen across portions of Iowa, and eventually move into Northern \nIllinois by mid-day Wednesday. Temperatures Wednesday will rise \nclose to 60 degrees, with warmer temperatures for our southern \ncounties by midday, but will change as a cold front from the north \nplows through in the afternoon hours. In addition, the tight \npressure gradient across Missouri will result in higher winds of 15-\n20 kts, with localized gusts approaching 30 kts. Because of our \npressure gradient, we elected to include the NBM 90th percentile in \nour forecast to account for higher wind chances. In terms of \nprecipitation, a low chance for a stray shower is possible, \nespecially for our far northeast counties. However, forcing and \nmoisture totals needed are aligned much better to our east. Winds \ndiminish during the late evening and overnight hours Wednesday into \nThursday, as the low continues to progress east.\n\nWinds Thursday will continue to come from the north, and as a result, \ncooler temperatures in the 40s and low 50s be the reality during the \nday. Overnight Thursday, skies will be mostly clear, in which the \nNBM models are giving high probabilities of temperatures below 32 \ndegrees, especially in areas north of Interstate 70, and outside of \nKansas City proper. Cold air advection will remain on Friday, which \nwill help promote higher probabilities of a widespread freeze for \nSaturday morning.",
